#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184932} :activity/id #uuid "23370b95-0255-56e2-941f-aa45341f5a48" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050044} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185383 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, **completing the square** on a quadratic expression means expressing it as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to complete the square on a quadratic expression when the coefficient of the linear term is odd. In these cases, we get a fraction in the final expression.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 5x + \\left(\\dfrac 52\\right)^2\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the large parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+5x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "858a20e8-2e9b-5ab0-ae5f-811c370a9b56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9421 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dde3630a-2987-5c08-902d-908c2a28f36d" :step/math-academy-id 183235 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183961 :question/id #uuid "fc7c982c-7e5d-5187-9779-6952f32fe2bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-15019" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3\n$$\n\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+7x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701722} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705070}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated"} :step/id #uuid "8c7c2830-c121-536d-9010-2a49fcebeeb7" :step/math-academy-id 183236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185384 :tutorial/content "Let's complete the square on the following expression: \n\n$$\nx^2 + 3x + 4\n$$\n\nThe procedure is very similar to the one we encountered when the coefficient of the linear term was even.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dba1a2d3-8092-514c-905c-9f6c001081ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9422 :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "e2ab26c1-628c-5277-a285-79d9416fcf17" :step/math-academy-id 183239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183962 :question/id #uuid "05109436-7f8b-5ef9-a460-b53497a138f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15020" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{53}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705329} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705351}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} :step/id #uuid "3bd99ae4-941e-5eb1-9251-6d5c1c10ce22" :step/math-academy-id 183240 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183963 :question/id #uuid "2308b4bc-c09f-5f4c-b879-3bb67c92f885" :question/math-academy-id "e-15021" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{49}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701850} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705130}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"} :step/id #uuid "5c579b82-a690-58c4-a13a-883a3fdebaae" :step/math-academy-id 183243}] :activity/title "Completing the Square With Odd Linear Terms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184426}]}
